    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=3$, giving $y=3x+1$, where\n$$x\\le-2-2\\sqrt3\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x\\ge-2+2\\sqrt3.$$\nThe parameter has $t\\ne-1$. Find the range of $x$ using the discriminant of the quadratic in $t$.",
      "worked": "For $y=mx+b$, multiplying by $t+1$ gives\n$$at^2+t+7=m(t^2+2)+b(t+1).$$\nCompare coefficients:\n$$\\begin{align*}a&=m\\\\1&=b\\\\7&=2m+b.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $m=3$, $b=1$ and $a=3$, so $y=3x+1$.\n\nThe original expressions require $t\\ne-1$. From the expression for $x$,\n$$t^2-xt+2-x=0.$$\nFor real $t$, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(-x)^2-4(2-x)\\\\\\Delta&=x^2+4x-8\\\\\\Delta&=(x+2)^2-12.\\end{align*}$$\nThus\n$$(x+2)^2\\ge12,$$\nso\n$$x\\le-2-2\\sqrt3\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x\\ge-2+2\\sqrt3.$$\nSubstituting $t=-1$ into the quadratic's left-hand side gives $3$, so its real roots never equal the excluded parameter. Both boundary values are attained.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
